WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:06.074
A customer writes: charged twice for one bike rental.

2
00:00:06.074 --> 00:00:09.066
Support wants past disputes that mean the same.

3
00:00:09.066 --> 00:00:14.859
An embedding model turns each text into a vector, and similar meanings land close together.

4
00:00:15.033 --> 00:00:20.011
The exact search measures the distance from the query to every vector: 40 here.

5
00:00:20.333 --> 00:00:28.361
With 50 million vectors of 1,536 numbers, that's 77 billion multiply-adds a query.

6
00:00:28.533 --> 00:00:30.887
HNSW gives up exactness for speed.

7
00:00:30.887 --> 00:00:37.741
It links every vector to a few of its nearest neighbours, and searches by walking toward the query.

8
00:00:37.967 --> 00:00:39.841
It also builds express lanes.

9
00:00:39.841 --> 00:00:45.980
A random few vectors sit in a layer above, with longer links, and fewer still in the top layer.

10
00:00:46.167 --> 00:00:49.201
The search starts at the entry point in the top layer.

11
00:00:49.201 --> 00:00:53.135
It measures its neighbours, and moves to the one closest to the query.

12
00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:57.275
Nothing there is closer, so it drops a layer, at the same vector.

13
00:00:57.467 --> 00:00:59.016
Layer 1 has shorter links.

14
00:00:59.016 --> 00:01:02.472
Two moves bring the search into the query's neighbourhood.

15
00:01:02.667 --> 00:01:04.980
The bottom layer holds every vector.

16
00:01:04.980 --> 00:01:10.957
Here it keeps a beam of the four best so far, and expands the closest until nothing improves.

17
00:01:11.233 --> 00:01:14.082
It stops after 17 distances instead of 40.

18
00:01:14.082 --> 00:01:18.355
But one of the four it returns is not a true nearest neighbour.

19
00:01:18.355 --> 00:01:20.661
This closer one was never reached.

20
00:01:20.867 --> 00:01:25.332
Recall is the share of the true nearest neighbours a search returns.

21
00:01:25.332 --> 00:01:29.403
Widen the beam to six, and it keeps expanding a little longer.

22
00:01:29.403 --> 00:01:31.767
It finds all four, for 22 distances.

23
00:01:32.033 --> 00:01:36.453
In pgvector the beam is ef search, 40 by default.

24
00:01:36.453 --> 00:01:50.073
On the lesson's 100,000 vectors, a beam of 10 found 89 percent of the true top ten, and 40 found them all, reading 654 pages to the exact scan's 2,138.

25
00:01:50.233 --> 00:01:56.287
Start at the sparse top, walk toward the query, and drop a layer each time you stop improving.

26
00:01:56.287 --> 00:01:59.185
A wider beam buys recall with more distances.
